Yes, but write it as a boolean expression, and fix the template check.

Current code has a bug/mismatch:

```html
@if (showGlobalBack !== 'HIDDEN' && testLoaded) {
```

`showGlobalBack` is boolean, so it will never equal `'HIDDEN'`.

You can inline it like this:

```ts
protected showGlobalBack: boolean =
  this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_backward_button !== undefined &&
  this.tcs.booklet.config.navbar_backward_button !== 'HIDDEN';
```

Then template:

```html
@if (showGlobalBack && testLoaded) {
```

But important: this is evaluated once when the component is constructed. If `tcs.booklet` is loaded later, `showGlobalBack` will stay `false`. In that case, either assign it after booklet load, or use a getter:

```ts
protected get showGlobalBack(): boolean {
  return this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_backward_button !== undefined &&
    this.tcs.booklet.config.navbar_backward_button !== 'HIDDEN';
}
```

For this case, getter is likely cleaner/minimal.

